1094
00:44:14.960 --> 00:44:16.550
or even, you know, like, I mean.

1095
00:44:16.550 --> 00:44:20.770
But periodization is a tried and true approach

1096
00:44:21.980 --> 00:44:22.850
to training for any.

1097
00:44:22.850 --> 00:44:24.960
It doesn't have to be an ultra distance thing.

1098
00:44:24.960 --> 00:44:26.290
If you're doing Olympic triathlons.

1099
00:44:26.290 --> 00:44:27.500
If you're training for a marathon,

1100
00:44:27.500 --> 00:44:31.350
if you're training for any kind of physical feat

1101
00:44:31.350 --> 00:44:33.140
or endurance challenge.

1102
00:44:33.140 --> 00:44:37.870
Periodization is a way of structuring how you create

1103
00:44:37.870 --> 00:44:39.510
your schedule going forward.

1104
00:44:39.510 --> 00:44:42.793
So let's say you have a year to prepare for a race,

1105
00:44:42.793 --> 00:44:45.610
a really big race, it's on the calendar.

1106
00:44:45.610 --> 00:44:48.620
You look at the end date and you're like, okay,

1107
00:44:48.620 --> 00:44:49.480
that day is coming,

1108
00:44:49.480 --> 00:44:52.540
Here I am 365 days beforehand.

1109
00:44:52.540 --> 00:44:55.717
How am I going to best prepare so that on that race day

1110
00:44:55.717 --> 00:45:00.717
I'm at my absolute peak to perform to my capabilities.

1111
00:45:01.970 --> 00:45:06.210
And the way you do that is you break down your training

1112
00:45:06.210 --> 00:45:07.980
into certain blocks.

1113
00:45:07.980 --> 00:45:11.050
You have this build phase and then you back off

1114
00:45:11.050 --> 00:45:12.680
and then you go a little bit harder

1115
00:45:12.680 --> 00:45:16.480
and then you back off again and then you increase again.

1116
00:45:16.480 --> 00:45:19.440
And then maybe you take two weeks of really easy training.

1117
00:45:19.440 --> 00:45:23.630
Like you build in periods of time mindfully

1118
00:45:23.630 --> 00:45:25.890
that allow your body to recover.

1119
00:45:25.890 --> 00:45:29.600
And this can happen quarterly, it can happen monthly.

1120
00:45:29.600 --> 00:45:31.730
It happens weekly. It happens daily.

1121
00:45:31.730 --> 00:45:34.410
So there's the macro and the micro that gets built

1122
00:45:34.410 --> 00:45:35.840
into all of this.

1123
00:45:35.840 --> 00:45:40.840
So that you are breaking your body down but not blind

1124
00:45:43.800 --> 00:45:45.830
to the fact that you've got to allow your body

1125
00:45:45.830 --> 00:45:48.660
to build itself back up so that you can then

1126
00:45:48.660 --> 00:45:51.340
approach your training as a stronger, fitter athlete

1127
00:45:51.340 --> 00:45:53.560
to take it, you know, to the next level.

1128
00:45:53.560 --> 00:45:55.600
To break through certain plateaus.

1129
00:45:55.600 --> 00:45:58.160
And knowing when to back off and how to back off

1130
00:45:58.160 --> 00:46:01.810
and for how long to back off is both an art and a science.

1131
00:46:01.810 --> 00:46:05.010
And I've had my best years as an athlete,

1132
00:46:05.010 --> 00:46:08.780
my best performances when I've really taken periodization

1133
00:46:08.780 --> 00:46:13.740
to heart and follow the direction of my coach, Chris South.

1134
00:46:13.740 --> 00:46:16.090
When I was a swimmer, there was no periodization,

1135
00:46:16.090 --> 00:46:18.570
we just went in and we hammered every single day.

1136
00:46:18.570 --> 00:46:20.710
And then we hung, you know, all our hopes

1137
00:46:20.710 --> 00:46:23.410
on a two week taper at the end of the season

1138
00:46:23.410 --> 00:46:25.050
and just prayed that it all worked out.

1139
00:46:25.050 --> 00:46:26.857
Sometimes it did. Sometimes it didn't.

1140
00:46:26.857 --> 00:46:29.407
But you were, that was early, earlier days.

1141
00:46:29.407 --> 00:46:30.550
This is like 80's.

1142
00:46:30.550 --> 00:46:31.383
That's what they did then.

1143
00:46:31.383 --> 00:46:32.216
Right, exactly.

1144
00:46:32.216 --> 00:46:33.049
It was all about...

1145
00:46:33.049 --> 00:46:37.910
But now, it's like understanding how hard you can push

1146
00:46:37.910 --> 00:46:40.320
before you tip into over-training and knowing

1147
00:46:40.320 --> 00:46:43.490
when to back off the gas and allowing your body to recover.

1148
00:46:43.490 --> 00:46:47.280
Knowing how long you need for your body to bounce back.

1149
00:46:47.280 --> 00:46:49.180
And what kind of training you're doing

1150
00:46:49.180 --> 00:46:53.390
during those sort of easier intervals of time

1151
00:46:53.390 --> 00:46:56.625
really becomes this incredible lever for growth

1152
00:46:56.625 --> 00:46:59.320
and performance breakthroughs.

1153
00:46:59.320 --> 00:47:04.210
So how would you, like if you're training for Catalina,

1154
00:47:04.210 --> 00:47:07.120
I'm most likely not doing the full distance Catalina

1155
00:47:07.120 --> 00:47:10.300
because I can't commit to the time it's gonna take to train

1156
00:47:10.300 --> 00:47:12.580
for it with a young one.

1157
00:47:12.580 --> 00:47:15.910
But I'm gonna do the sprint, which is 15 K.

1158
00:47:15.910 --> 00:47:17.730
So how would you periodize that?

1159
00:47:17.730 --> 00:47:20.410
Like when would you start and how would you periodize that?

1160
00:47:20.410 --> 00:47:22.610
First I would break up the period of time

1161
00:47:22.610 --> 00:47:24.130
into like three phases.

1162
00:47:24.130 --> 00:47:27.530
The first phase would be endurance building

1163
00:47:27.530 --> 00:47:29.020
like base-building period.

1164
00:47:29.020 --> 00:47:32.170
Where you're just working on your foundation

1165
00:47:32.170 --> 00:47:34.980
and you're getting a lot of zone to work in

1166
00:47:34.980 --> 00:47:38.570
and getting acclimated to spending a lot of time

1167
00:47:38.570 --> 00:47:40.680
swimming and running, so that your body

1168
00:47:42.080 --> 00:47:43.460
kind of knows what that feels like.

1169
00:47:43.460 --> 00:47:45.440
You're giving your tendons and your ligaments

1170
00:47:45.440 --> 00:47:48.370
the opportunity to adapt, not just your lungs

1171
00:47:48.370 --> 00:47:51.100
and your heart, but all of your muscles in your body

1172
00:47:51.100 --> 00:47:53.930
to figure out, like how to sustain itself

1173
00:47:53.930 --> 00:47:55.080
over long periods of time.

1174
00:47:55.080 --> 00:47:58.350
So that phase would be a slow progressive build

1175
00:47:58.350 --> 00:48:01.020
of going longer and longer and longer.

1176
00:48:01.020 --> 00:48:03.420
Mostly in the zone two phase.

1177
00:48:03.420 --> 00:48:05.870
But within that block, you're gonna do a little,

1178
00:48:05.870 --> 00:48:07.870
maybe like a two week build and then you're gonna have

1179
00:48:07.870 --> 00:48:10.610
four days that are gonna be pretty easy or maybe a week

1180
00:48:10.610 --> 00:48:13.150
that's pretty easy, where you're still training

1181
00:48:13.150 --> 00:48:16.750
but the gas isn't all the way pressed down, right?

1182
00:48:16.750 --> 00:48:21.750
You're backing off so that during that week of training,

1183
00:48:22.012 --> 00:48:24.780
you're allowing your body to recover and get stronger.

1184
00:48:24.780 --> 00:48:27.940
And then that would be followed by an another sub block

1185
00:48:27.940 --> 00:48:31.780
within the base building block of going a little bit longer

1186
00:48:31.780 --> 00:48:33.890
than you did in that initial block,

1187
00:48:33.890 --> 00:48:35.500
to push through to the next barrier.

1188
00:48:35.500 --> 00:48:38.210
And you just repeat, rinse and repeat, rinse and repeat

1189
00:48:38.210 --> 00:48:40.500
and pay attention to how your body is recovering

1190
00:48:40.500 --> 00:48:44.500
during those off periods so that you can better calibrate

1191
00:48:44.500 --> 00:48:46.490
what those rest weeks look like.

1192
00:48:46.490 --> 00:48:49.610
And then within each week you have a rest day, right?

1193
00:48:49.610 --> 00:48:52.070
So, and then within each day, you know, you have,

1194
00:48:52.070 --> 00:48:54.085
when are you training and what are you doing

1195
00:48:54.085 --> 00:48:55.720
in between your training sessions?

1196
00:48:55.720 --> 00:48:58.900
So periodization works at every level.

1197
00:48:58.900 --> 00:49:03.800
From yearly to monthly, to, you know, weekly to daily.

1198
00:49:03.800 --> 00:49:07.230
Then your second phase would be more of a strength

1199
00:49:07.230 --> 00:49:09.320
and speed building phase.

1200
00:49:09.320 --> 00:49:11.750
Where you're going to be doing more tempo work interspersed

1201
00:49:11.750 --> 00:49:14.880
with continuing to increase your volume over zone two.

1202
00:49:14.880 --> 00:49:17.407
But you're gonna choose moments where you're gonna have

1203
00:49:17.407 --> 00:49:22.407
really hard kind of zone three zone four, zone five efforts

1204
00:49:23.130 --> 00:49:27.140
in there to get used to the idea that your experience

1205
00:49:27.140 --> 00:49:28.090
when you're doing a swim run.

1206
00:49:28.090 --> 00:49:30.061
Which is that you're always changing gears.

1207
00:49:30.061 --> 00:49:32.290
You're ramping up your heart rate, it's coming back down,

1208
00:49:32.290 --> 00:49:34.530
it's going about, you're like, it's not like an Ironman

1209
00:49:34.530 --> 00:49:36.210
or a marathon where you get into,

1210
00:49:36.210 --> 00:49:38.290
you rev up to a certain speed and you hold it

1211
00:49:38.290 --> 00:49:39.250
as long as you can.

1212
00:49:39.250 --> 00:49:43.140
A small run, you're constantly switching back and forth.

1213
00:49:43.140 --> 00:49:46.180
So it would be more focused on getting your heart

1214
00:49:46.180 --> 00:49:48.160
and your lungs and your muscles acclimated

1215
00:49:48.160 --> 00:49:51.580
to heart rate spikes and getting fit enough

1216
00:49:51.580 --> 00:49:53.880
so that your heart rate comes back down

1217
00:49:53.880 --> 00:49:55.630
pretty expeditiously.

1218
00:49:55.630 --> 00:49:59.070
The third phase would be race specific,

1219
00:49:59.070 --> 00:50:02.280
then you're gonna start sort of training in the spirit

