of contributing their creativity in a manner

1288
00:53:08.600 --> 00:53:12.200
that's more sustainable with lifestyle.

1289
00:53:12.200 --> 00:53:15.004
It's interesting 'cause like two things came in my mind

1290
00:53:15.004 --> 00:53:16.360
when you told me you wanted to talk about this,

1291
00:53:16.360 --> 00:53:20.420
the first is when lonely planet travel guides

1292
00:53:20.420 --> 00:53:23.787
were my primary, you know, job, when I was doing that.

1293
00:53:23.787 --> 00:53:28.580
And it was a period from 2007 to 2013,

1294
00:53:28.580 --> 00:53:31.090
where I was gone eight, nine months a year.

1295
00:53:31.090 --> 00:53:33.130
And I would do like three of these guides

1296
00:53:33.130 --> 00:53:37.020
and that entailed long periods of travel,

1297
00:53:37.020 --> 00:53:41.649
usually in remote areas, not always, but usually.

1298
00:53:41.649 --> 00:53:45.450
And then come back either home or I'd rent a place

1299
00:53:45.450 --> 00:53:49.940
in the country and long periods of just, you know,

1300
00:53:49.940 --> 00:53:53.300
12 hour days, 10 hour days in the computer and then,

1301
00:53:53.300 --> 00:53:55.890
and living in situ, so I could make calls if I didn't have

1302
00:53:55.890 --> 00:53:59.300
all my research done, I knew where I was.

1303
00:53:59.300 --> 00:54:03.050
And so that has a built-in kind of, not fallow,

1304
00:54:03.050 --> 00:54:06.550
'cause you're still hustling, but there is periodization

1305
00:54:06.550 --> 00:54:09.280
in the research gathering and the writing.

1306
00:54:09.280 --> 00:54:11.290
And it felt natural.

1307
00:54:11.290 --> 00:54:15.236
And there is that still anytime you're on a story that like,

1308
00:54:15.236 --> 00:54:17.430
when you're trying to come up with something written

1309
00:54:17.430 --> 00:54:20.086
at the end of it versus what you're doing with a mics,

1310
00:54:20.086 --> 00:54:23.650
it's all one thing, but you're, you're reporting

1311
00:54:23.650 --> 00:54:24.890
but that's different than the writing.

1312
00:54:24.890 --> 00:54:27.320
Where you can analyze and be kind of create the distance

1313
00:54:27.320 --> 00:54:28.690
in between and organize it.

1314
00:54:28.690 --> 00:54:30.540
And you know what you want to say

1315
00:54:30.540 --> 00:54:34.010
versus the absorption which and the second thing

1316
00:54:34.010 --> 00:54:37.900
that came to mind was Bonnie's story which she published

1317
00:54:37.900 --> 00:54:41.410
in 2019, which I sent to you this morning.

1318
00:54:41.410 --> 00:54:44.020
And because that's what she's talking about

1319
00:54:44.020 --> 00:54:48.840
is creating that space for absorption as a creative person.

1320
00:54:48.840 --> 00:54:51.700
So that's immediately where my mind went.

1321
00:54:51.700 --> 00:54:54.940
It didn't go to athletics at all for me, because like,

1322
00:54:54.940 --> 00:54:57.830
athletics are just kind of like something

1323
00:54:57.830 --> 00:55:00.650
just to keep my mind right more than any than,

1324
00:55:00.650 --> 00:55:01.483
my body right.

1325
00:55:01.483 --> 00:55:02.590
But like, I don't think of it

1326
00:55:02.590 --> 00:55:05.513
in terms of any sort of outcomes or optimization.

1327
00:55:06.390 --> 00:55:07.370
But I immediately went to that point.

1328
00:55:07.370 --> 00:55:10.522
I was like, wow, I don't really have what I used to have.

1329
00:55:10.522 --> 00:55:14.670
There's no longer that period of like three months

1330
00:55:14.670 --> 00:55:17.320
where I'm just traveling and absorbing.

1331
00:55:17.320 --> 00:55:19.790
And three months of writing, like I don't have that anymore.

1332
00:55:19.790 --> 00:55:22.820
Two months of writing is what it used to be or a month.

1333
00:55:22.820 --> 00:55:23.653
So it's interesting.

1334
00:55:23.653 --> 00:55:25.100
And I did like that rhythm.

1335
00:55:25.100 --> 00:55:27.280
There's something real natural about it.

1336
00:55:27.280 --> 00:55:29.670
Yeah, it comes in phases, right?

1337
00:55:29.670 --> 00:55:31.320
Just like if you're making a movie,

1338
00:55:31.320 --> 00:55:34.300
you write the script by yourself or with a friend

1339
00:55:34.300 --> 00:55:38.170
or whatever, partner, then you shoot the movie,

1340
00:55:38.170 --> 00:55:41.030
then you edit the movie, then you market the movie.

1341
00:55:41.030 --> 00:55:43.800
All of those are part and parcel of being a filmmaker

1342
00:55:43.800 --> 00:55:45.710
but they're all very different disciplines

1343
00:55:45.710 --> 00:55:47.600
that I suspect for.

1344
00:55:47.600 --> 00:55:50.220
And, you know, every filmmaker is gonna excel

1345
00:55:50.220 --> 00:55:51.450
in different areas of those

1346
00:55:51.450 --> 00:55:53.290
and maybe doesn't like certain aspects of it.

1347
00:55:53.290 --> 00:55:55.510
But the switching gears aspect of it,

1348
00:55:55.510 --> 00:55:59.490
probably keeps them fresh, right?

1349
00:55:59.490 --> 00:56:01.400
In the same way that if you're a triathlete,

1350
00:56:01.400 --> 00:56:04.580
you get to switch up the different disciplines of sport

1351
00:56:04.580 --> 00:56:05.470
that you're pursuing.

1352
00:56:05.470 --> 00:56:06.560
For me people are always like,

1353
00:56:06.560 --> 00:56:07.700
which one do you like the best?

1354
00:56:07.700 --> 00:56:10.900
And it's like, I like that I get to change all the time.

1355
00:56:10.900 --> 00:56:13.750
And it's not the same thing every single day.

1356
00:56:13.750 --> 00:56:18.750
But I think on top of that is this idea of, you know,

1357
00:56:21.960 --> 00:56:25.130
to kind of harken to this idea of absorbing,

1358
00:56:25.130 --> 00:56:27.790
like that's where it gets tricky for people.

1359
00:56:27.790 --> 00:56:31.110
Like, look, a lot of people, they don't have the luxury

1360
00:56:31.110 --> 00:56:33.440
of like, I'm gonna kick back and absorb my life.

1361
00:56:33.440 --> 00:56:36.280
But if you're trying to express something,

1362
00:56:36.280 --> 00:56:39.530
if you're a creative person, you have to live your life

1363
00:56:39.530 --> 00:56:41.150
in order to have something to say,

1364
00:56:41.150 --> 00:56:43.010
you can't just be writing all the time.

1365
00:56:43.010 --> 00:56:44.720
Because you're gonna run out of things to write about

1366
00:56:44.720 --> 00:56:46.649
if you're not living, right?

1367
00:56:46.649 --> 00:56:47.483
Right.

1368
00:56:47.483 --> 00:56:49.140
So you have to live your life.

1369
00:56:49.140 --> 00:56:52.020
You have to have moments of absorption,

1370
00:56:52.020 --> 00:56:55.360
no matter what your career path or how busy

1371
00:56:55.360 --> 00:56:58.410
your circumstances may be.

1372
00:56:58.410 --> 00:57:00.740
What that looks like to you is gonna depend

1373
00:57:00.740 --> 00:57:03.540
on your circumstances and your, you know,

1374
00:57:03.540 --> 00:57:06.150
particularities of your life, of course.

1375
00:57:06.150 --> 00:57:07.810
But I think just being mindful of that

1376
00:57:07.810 --> 00:57:10.820
and trying to find ways, even within your day

1377
00:57:10.820 --> 00:57:15.820
in a given day to ruminate or do nothing

1378
00:57:15.820 --> 00:57:18.410
and not feel guilty about it, right?

1379
00:57:18.410 --> 00:57:19.243
Right.

1380
00:57:19.243 --> 00:57:21.380
There's that thing like stand-up comics

1381
00:57:21.380 --> 00:57:22.980
are people that just don't like to, you know,

1382
00:57:22.980 --> 00:57:25.097
like the people that don't like to wake up in the morning

1383
00:57:25.097 --> 00:57:28.240
you know, like are lazy, like become standup comics.

1384
00:57:28.240 --> 00:57:29.560
And it's like, what does that guy do?

1385
00:57:29.560 --> 00:57:30.470
He just does nothing.

1386
00:57:30.470 --> 00:57:33.780
He wakes up at noon and he like goes and gets a sandwich

1387
00:57:33.780 --> 00:57:35.020
and like wanders around.

1388
00:57:35.020 --> 00:57:39.640
And then does it's like, what is that person's, you know.

1389
00:57:39.640 --> 00:57:40.720
What's driving that person?

1390
00:57:40.720 --> 00:57:42.670
But they're overlooking the fact

1391
00:57:42.670 --> 00:57:44.300
that the brain is working all the time.

1392
00:57:44.300 --> 00:57:47.810
So there's observation, there's synthesis,

1393
00:57:47.810 --> 00:57:48.660
there's absorption.

1394
00:57:48.660 --> 00:57:50.210
All of those things are occurring

1395
00:57:50.210 --> 00:57:52.990
and we shouldn't be so quick to judge

1396
00:57:52.990 --> 00:57:55.760
how certain human operates.

1397
00:57:55.760 --> 00:57:59.000
Also, it's funny we say, he's doing nothing

1398
00:57:59.000 --> 00:58:02.230
or she's doing nothing, but they're being.

1399
00:58:02.230 --> 00:58:03.488
You know, it's almost like...

1400
00:58:03.488 --> 00:58:05.200
Right, but we're not allowed to just be

1401
00:58:05.200 --> 00:58:07.019
that's the broader point, right?

1402
00:58:07.019 --> 00:58:07.852
Right.

1403
00:58:07.852 --> 00:58:11.310
I mean there is a certain element of like, I think you get

1404
00:58:11.310 --> 00:58:13.240
to a certain point where you have to be able to afford

1405
00:58:13.240 --> 00:58:15.413
to be, but everyone has that.

1406
00:58:15.413 --> 00:58:18.000
Like I don't care what your job is.

1407
00:58:18.000 --> 00:58:20.770
There's going to be pressures too.

1408
00:58:20.770 --> 00:58:23.030
And we often fill that space of doing nothing

1409
00:58:23.030 --> 00:58:25.000
with absorbing other people's content.

1410
00:58:25.000 --> 00:58:27.820
Like most people I think absorb it through television

1411
00:58:27.820 --> 00:58:30.140
or Netflix or YouTube or whatever

1412
00:58:33.000 --> 00:58:35.050
and you know, myself included.

1413
00:58:35.050 --> 00:58:38.170
But you know, what Bonnie is talking about here

1414
00:58:38.170 --> 00:58:40.681
is kind of an active, not doing,

1415
00:58:40.681 --> 00:58:43.740
it's going to the park and people watching, it's reading,

1416
00:58:43.740 --> 00:58:45.463
she's not really advocating.

1417
00:58:47.320 --> 00:58:49.380
She's not really advocating vegging on the couch

1418
00:58:49.380 --> 00:58:50.520
and watching television.

1419
00:58:50.520 --> 00:58:51.489
No, no, no.

1420
00:58:51.489 --> 00:58:54.270
She's separating out the fallow, yeah.

1421
00:58:54.270 --> 00:58:58.410
Yeah, and acknowledging that in this age of distraction

1422
00:58:58.410 --> 00:59:00.760
that it's transgressive to do this, right?

1423
00:59:00.760 --> 00:59:03.710
There's one quote that I noted that I wanted to read

1424
00:59:03.710 --> 00:59:06.230
from the piece, which is called, "you're doing something

1425
00:59:06.230 --> 00:59:08.300
important when you aren't doing anything", we'll link it up

1426
00:59:08.300 --> 00:59:11.650
in the show notes, but she says, "protecting and practicing

1427
00:59:11.650 --> 00:59:14.040
fallow time is an act of resistance.

1428
00:59:14.040 --> 00:59:16.380
It can make us feel out of step with what the prevailing

1429
00:59:16.380 --> 00:59:19.650
culture tells us, the 24/7 hamster wheel of work,

1430
00:59:19.650 --> 00:59:22.230
the constant accessibility and the impatient press

1431
00:59:22.230 --> 00:59:23.720
of social media.

1432
00:59:23.720 --> 00:59:26.620
All hasten the anxiety over someone else's judgment.

1433
00:59:26.620 --> 00:59:30.080
If you aren't visibly producing you aren't worthy.

1434
00:59:30.080 --> 00:59:33.180
In this context taking time to lie dormant feels greedy,

1435
00:59:33.180 --> 00:59:35.760
even wasteful, and of course there are often

1436
00:59:35.760 --> 00:59:37.500
financial concerns."

1437
00:59:37.500 --> 00:59:40.010
So yeah, this notion that it's transgressive,

1438
00:59:40.010 --> 00:59:42.860
that it is an act of resistance that you have to push back

1439
00:59:42.860 --> 00:59:46.010
against culture and your addictive instinct to grab

1440
00:59:46.010 --> 00:59:50.733
the phone and ameliorate your, you know, your boredom

1441
00:59:50.733 --> 00:59:53.483
by filling it with scrolling or whatever it is

1442
00:59:53.483 --> 00:59:56.000
that you look out on your phone, it's very difficult.

1443
00:59:56.000 --> 00:59:59.560
It's never been more difficult to carve out that time,

1444
00:59:59.560 --> 01:00:02.760
that fallow time, time for rumination and quiethood.

1445
01:00:02.760 --> 01:00:03.700
And feeling wasteful.

1446
01:00:03.700 --> 01:00:05.070
I mean, yeah, that's exactly it.

1447
01:00:05.070 --> 01:00:08.860
Like, am I wasting time by doing X or Y or whatever.

1448
01:00:08.860 --> 01:00:12.743
And, you know, that's one thing that I think adventuring,

1449
01:00:14.790 --> 01:00:17.220
which is what I consider swimming and running these days

1450
01:00:17.220 --> 01:00:20.320
for me, it's like these mini adventures

1451
01:00:20.320 --> 01:00:22.370
that allows me to clear my head in a way,

1452
01:00:22.370 --> 01:00:24.980
that's a fallow time within a day for me.

1453
01:00:24.980 --> 01:00:26.550
But it's not though, because I'm still trying

1454
01:00:26.550 --> 01:00:28.180
to get something done.

1455
01:00:28.180 --> 01:00:31.202
So it really doesn't qualify as fallow time.

1456
01:00:31.202 --> 01:00:32.449
Yeah. It's hard.

1457
01:00:32.449 --> 01:00:33.282
I know.

1458
01:00:33.282 --> 01:00:35.390
And it's pushing my son and is like, dude,

1459
01:00:35.390 --> 01:00:37.259
tricycle is kind of fallow time.

1460
01:00:37.259 --> 01:00:38.610
But I also think it's the mindset that you bring

1461
01:00:38.610 --> 01:00:40.820
to whatever it is that you're doing, right?

1462
01:00:40.820 --> 01:00:45.193
Like you could go out and go for a walk with your son

1463
01:00:45.193 --> 01:00:49.380
and bring your phone and stare at your phone the whole time.

1464
01:00:49.380 --> 01:00:53.740
Or you leave your phone at home and you comport yourself

1465
01:00:53.740 --> 01:00:55.830
like Brogan and go up and talk to people

1466
01:00:55.830 --> 01:00:59.870
and live in your brain and, you know, kind of allow yourself

1467
01:00:59.870 --> 01:01:01.880
to mentally meander.

1468
01:01:01.880 --> 01:01:05.013
Yeah, which, you know,

1469
01:01:06.430 --> 01:01:08.847
we've been watching "Mad men" 'cause April never seen

1470
01:01:08.847 --> 01:01:11.080
"Mad men", so I'm rewatching the whole series.

1471
01:01:11.080 --> 01:01:12.540
We're in season six now.

1472
01:01:12.540 --> 01:01:15.560
And that's something, for all of Don Draper's many faults

1473
01:01:15.560 --> 01:01:17.280
although he is gifted too

1474
01:01:18.810 --> 01:01:21.590
One thing that we love about Don is his willingness to nap

1475
01:01:21.590 --> 01:01:22.990
at any time of day or night.

1476
01:01:22.990 --> 01:01:25.300
He is a world-class napper.

1477
01:01:25.300 --> 01:01:28.700
But when you're boozing that hard

1478
01:01:28.700 --> 01:01:30.420
I don't see any other way around it.

1479
01:01:30.420 --> 01:01:34.080
Like when you're hammering like four Manhattans at lunch,

1480
01:01:34.080 --> 01:01:36.420
a nap is in your immediate future.

1481
01:01:36.420 --> 01:01:38.410
And nobody loves a matinee more than Don Draper

1482
01:01:38.410 --> 01:01:39.243
on a workday.

1483
01:01:39.243 --> 01:01:40.500
That's true.

1484
01:01:40.500 --> 01:01:41.940
But that's exactly what he's doing though.

1485
01:01:41.940 --> 01:01:42.980
He calls that absorption.

1486
01:01:42.980 --> 01:01:45.180
He would be fully in on this.

1487
01:01:45.180 --> 01:01:46.610
Like the absorption state,

1488
01:01:46.610 --> 01:01:48.413
he didn't care when people worked.

1489
01:01:49.295 --> 01:01:51.790
You never see him in front of a whiteboard

1490
01:01:51.790 --> 01:01:56.210
mapping out his strategy for, you know, brand X.

1491
01:01:56.210 --> 01:01:59.040
He kind of comes up with his ideas sua sponte,

1492
01:01:59.040 --> 01:02:01.570
like very extemporaneously in the meeting.

1493
01:02:01.570 --> 01:02:05.403
Yeah, I am more like the Peggy Olsen, sweating it out.

1494
01:02:06.339 --> 01:02:08.750
Like trying to hammer at it.

1495
01:02:08.750 --> 01:02:09.583
Figure it out.

1496
01:02:09.583 --> 01:02:13.570
I'm not the Don Draper nappy matinee genius.

1497
01:02:13.570 --> 01:02:14.970
That's funny.

1498
01:02:14.970 --> 01:02:15.803
Does it hold up?

1499
01:02:15.803 --> 01:02:17.420
I bet it holds up pretty good. Right?

1500
01:02:17.420 --> 01:02:19.100
There was one episode where they had to do

1501
01:02:19.100 --> 01:02:20.990
a trigger warning though.

1502
01:02:20.990 --> 01:02:21.890
Really?
Yeah.

1503
01:02:22.840 --> 01:02:23.780
Even though it takes place in the 60's.

1504
01:02:23.780 --> 01:02:24.613
Which one?

1505
01:02:24.613 --> 01:02:27.628
It was when Roger sang in Blackface.

1506
01:02:27.628 --> 01:02:28.461
Oh.

1507
01:02:28.461 --> 01:02:29.780
And they did a trigger warning.

1508
01:02:29.780 --> 01:02:33.170
Roger, not that politically correct that guy.

1509
01:02:33.170 --> 01:02:34.250
Politically incorrect.

1510
01:02:34.250 --> 01:02:35.460
Well, you know, where they were early

1511
01:02:35.460 --> 01:02:37.540
in like the first season, it was 1960.

1512
01:02:37.540 --> 01:02:40.237
And they were all on Nixon side.

1513
01:02:40.237 --> 01:02:41.232
I remember that.

1514
01:02:41.232 --> 01:02:43.410
Yeah, I remember that, because they were considering

1515
01:02:43.410 --> 01:02:45.910
getting into the campaign marketing.

1516
01:02:45.910 --> 01:02:47.500
They wrapped Nixon, but Nixon wouldn't,

1517
01:02:47.500 --> 01:02:49.154
like the campaign wasn't listening to them.

1518
01:02:49.154 --> 01:02:51.640
(both laughing)

1519
01:02:51.640 --> 01:02:53.517
All right, I think we got off track here.

1520
01:02:53.517 --> 01:02:56.590
Did we cover the periodization of your life?

1521
01:02:56.590 --> 01:02:57.840
Tell me about your life.

1522
01:02:57.840 --> 01:02:59.670
Like periodizing, like where are you

1523
01:02:59.670 --> 01:03:01.540
in your periodization journey?

1524
01:03:01.540 --> 01:03:05.840
And what's the most successful you've ever been at it?

1525
01:03:05.840 --> 01:03:08.650
Well, one thing I've done is to build in this month

1526
01:03:08.650 --> 01:03:11.500
every year where I take off, which is new,

1527
01:03:11.500 --> 01:03:12.890
I've done it twice.

1528
01:03:12.890 --> 01:03:15.180
But I intend to do that every year.

1529
01:03:15.180 --> 01:03:19.040
I think travel for me is a big aspect of that,

1530
01:03:19.040 --> 01:03:22.340
in terms of rejuvenating me and exposing me

1531
01:03:22.340 --> 01:03:24.660
to new people and new places.

1532
01:03:24.660 --> 01:03:26.763
I find that to be very nourishing.

1533
01:03:27.718 --> 01:03:30.980
Like your big Island.

1534
01:03:30.980 --> 01:03:34.900
Yeah, when I went to Australia, but even, you know,

1535
01:03:34.900 --> 01:03:37.920
when pre-COVID there'd be speaking things

1536
01:03:37.920 --> 01:03:39.680
and I would get to go to all these cool places.

1537
01:03:39.680 --> 01:03:43.070
And for me, that like really reboots my system.

1538
01:03:43.070 --> 01:03:45.900
So COVID has been challenging just to be at home

